{{- /* rotini markdown-reference template (default — edit freely). Renders the
       SAME per-command doc-data as help/man as one markdown reference page per
       command. No tab columns here: markdown lists render better than tabwriter
       alignment in a .md file. After rendering, rotini trims trailing whitespace,
       collapses blank-line runs, and drops the trailing newline.

       DATA: .Header .Summary .Description .Footer .Invocation
         .Usage/.UsageDerived .Passthrough .CommandGroups[] .Arguments[] .FlagGroups[]
         .Environment[] .Configuration[] .Cascading[] .Examples[] .ExampleRows[] .Stdin .Output
         .ExitStatus[] .SeeAlso[] .SeeAlsoRows[] .Files[] .FlagRules[] .Metadata .PageName .ParentPage
         (.Stdin and .Output are nil when the command declares none). .PageName is the page's
         name (taskr-add), the file a markdown install_dir writes as <PageName>.md, and
         .ParentPage the parent command's ("" on the root's page); command and topic rows carry
         .Page, their own, which the default template links as <Page>.md. .Files lists the
         configuration files the command's inputs are read from, in the order they win, as
         {.File .Format .Found .PathFrom .AsEnv .Profiles .Note .Name} (nil when none of its
         inputs reads one): .File is the path, or the name discovery looks for, .Note the rest
         in one line (see help.txt.tmpl). .FlagRules are the rules between the command's flags,
         {.Kind .Flags .Condition .Text}, .Text a sentence such as "--json and --yaml can't be
         used together". A config row carries .Key (what a user writes in the file) and .File
         (the one file it is read from, "" for any), beside the spec's .Name and .Location.
         .Metadata, on the page and on command, argument, flag, env and config rows, is the
         item's own `metadata` labels as written; the default template doesn't show it. A flag,
         env or config row's .Type is the value's label in the user's words (string, int,
         number, duration, file, string... for a list, key=value for a map, value for a type of
         the author's own; see help.txt.tmpl). .Passthrough is the command's name when every word after it is passed
         through unparsed, else "". A command or flag group carries .Description, the
         paragraph the command's `groups` gives it.
         A flag or argument row also carries .Env (its environment variables, in lookup order) and
         .ConfigKey (its config key, set only when the command reads config files);
         alsoSetBy .Env .ConfigKey "`" "`" renders them as "also set by ...".
         Argument, flag, env and config rows carry .Constraints (the declared bounds, lengths,
         item counts and repeatability as a short note) and .Rules (the same as sentences,
         plus pattern and separator), and .Accepts (what a time or integer input accepts beyond its type's
         default: a layout, relative times, base prefixes).
         Argument, flag, env and config rows carry .EnumValues: every value that is not hidden,
         in declared order, as {.Value .Summary .Aliases .Deprecated .DeprecatedSince .RemovedIn
         .ReplacedBy .Note}, nil when no value declares more than its spelling (.Enum always
         holds the values alone, hidden ones left out); listed .Enum .EnumValues leaves out the
         deprecated ones too. An argument row carries .Passthrough when it and every word after it are
         passed on as typed, and .Placeholder, its declared display name ("" for none). An output carries .Stream when the command writes a stream of
         .Type, one item at a time. An exit status row carries .Name (its snake_case name, or
         "") and .Retryable. Command, argument, flag, env and config rows carry
         .DeprecatedSince and .RemovedIn; deprecation .Deprecated .DeprecatedSince .RemovedIn
         renders "deprecated since 1.4.0, removed in 2.0.0: <message>". The page's own
         .Deprecated is the command's deprecation message, ending "; use <replacement> instead"
         when it declares replaced_by ("" when it is not deprecated), with .DeprecatedSince and
         .RemovedIn. .ExitStatus is the command's exit_status, else the root's, which is
         program-wide. A row's .Default writes a list or map default as JSON.
         Argument, flag, env and config rows carry .Description (the input's longer text,
         paragraphs separated by blank lines) and .Stability ("experimental", "beta" or "" for
         stable, as declared), and command rows carry .Stability. The page's own .Stability is
         the command's, inherited from its ancestors; stabilityNote .Stability renders the line
         saying what it promises. An exit status row carries .DocsURL, a link to more about the
         code, or "".
         The page's .Effects says what running the command does, as declared ("destructive,
         not idempotent, open world"), "" when it declares none; a flag row's .Effects says what
         giving the flag adds ("destructive").
         The root's page carries .Topics[] {.Name .Summary}, its help topics, and .ResponseFiles,
         the response-file prefix ("" when response files are off). On a topic's page
         .Topic is true: .Summary and .Description are the topic's, .Environment holds a
         generated environment topic's rows, .Files and .Configuration a generated configuration
         topic's, each row with .Commands (the commands that read it), and the rest is empty.
       HELPERS: join, upper, lower, title, trim, trimPrefix, trimSuffix, replace,
                indent, repeat, default, contains, hasPrefix, hasSuffix, first, last,
                alsoSetBy, deprecation, stabilityNote, listed, described, code, notes (the
                non-empty notes in one parenthetical: " (a, b)"). */ -}}
